"Surah al-Ghashiyah takes its name from the word 'al-Ghashiyah', which means 'the covering, the all-encompassing' and refers to the terrifying event of the Resurrection that will engulf everything. This surah urges attentive listening to that momentous news and explains what a great calamity the Resurrection will be for the disbelievers, and what an eternal bliss it will be for the believers.\n\nOn that Day, some faces will be humbled, downcast in humiliation. These are people who did not strive in the world upon the path commanded by Allah, but instead wore themselves out for falsehood and labored in vain. According to different interpretations, this fatigue and distress is either the very torment of Hell in the Hereafter, or the retribution for their wasted deeds in the world. In the end, all of them are in loss.\n\nThese people will enter a scorching Fire and be made to drink from a boiling spring. Their food will be a poisonous, thorny plant called 'dari'', which neither nourishes nor satisfies hunger. This punishment is the bitter consequence of their having toiled in vain and failed to strive in the way of truth.\n\nOther faces on that Day, however, will be in bliss and joy. These are those who strove in the world in accordance with the pleasure of Allah, saw the reward of their deeds, and were well-pleased with what they had done. They will be in an elevated Paradise; no vain or futile talk is heard therein, but only peace and happiness.\n\nIn Paradise, there is a flowing spring, raised couches, cups filled with drink, cushions arranged in rows, and splendid carpets spread out. These blessings have been prepared for the believers who performed righteous deeds in the world and earned the pleasure of Allah.\n\nAllah invites people to look at the lessons in creation: Do they not look at the camel, how it was created; at the sky, how it was raised high; and at the mountains, how they were set firm? These beings are a means to contemplate Allah's power and the Day of the Hereafter. Through this reflection, man can find the right path and attain eternal happiness.\n\nAlmighty Allah asks people to look at the earth and the order upon it. They are commanded to ponder how the earth has been spread out, how the mountains, plains, and seas were created, and how it was made suitable for human life. This perspective is at the same time a means to comprehend that resurrection after death, reckoning, and retribution are real, and to recognize the power of Allah.\n\nPeople will live on the earth for a while, then die and be resurrected. For this reason, they must know the place in which they dwell, observe the fate of past nations, and spend their lives in faith, spiritual discernment, and righteous deeds. By commanding that one look at such physical evidence, the Quran points to the importance of engaging in rational and empirical sciences such as astronomy and geology. However, these sciences should not be used solely for worldly purposes, but to recognize Allah's signs and prepare for the Hereafter.\n\nThe expression 'Do they not look?' occurring in the verse reproaches those who do not pay attention to such admonitory matters. Allah has left the details of creation in this universe to human investigation, having provided a comprehensive reminder. This reminder opens the door to attaining deeper knowledge by employing reason and empirical observation.\n\nO Muhammad! You are only a reminder; you are not tasked with compelling them to believe. Give counsel and admonition, but do not compel; for ruling over the hearts belongs to Allah.\n\nWhoever turns away from the reminders and persists in disbelief, Allah will punish him with the greatest torment. This torment is the eternal punishment in the Hereafter, compared to which the punishment of this world is minor. In the end, everyone will return to Allah and give account; there is no escape from this reckoning."